<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Replaces the blanket android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" that previously
    lived on <application>. That flag permitted plaintext HTTP to ANY host
    reachable from the app. This config still permits plaintext HTTP to any
    host, for the same reason the flag did: Xtream Codes provider hostnames
    are entered by the user at runtime (there is no fixed backend), so a
    build-time domain allowlist is not possible without breaking real
    providers, most of which only serve plain HTTP.

    Honest accounting of what this buys over the old flag:
      - It does NOT restrict which hosts may be contacted over cleartext.
        cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" on the base-config applies to all
        domains, same as before.
      - It DOES give the app an explicit trust-anchor declaration
        (system CAs only) for TLS/HTTPS connections, and a single place to
        tighten later (e.g. per-domain <domain-config> overrides, or
        user-configurable pinning) once provider hosts are known at
        install/runtime and a UI exists to manage that trust.
      - It also documents intent: this is a deliberate, scoped decision
        rather than an accidental leftover flag, and centralizes future
        network security policy in one reviewable file instead of a manifest
        attribute.

    Residual risk: plaintext traffic to the user-entered Xtream host (and to
    any other host the app happens to talk to) can still be observed or
    tampered with by a network-level attacker (e.g. on hostile Wi-Fi). This
    is inherent to supporting HTTP-only IPTV providers and is not solved by
    this file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system"/>
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
